#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240891 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175963 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9dc69a9e-6344-5a92-beb7-0c9fef8171d3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Cubic Equation With Complex Roots Given One Complex Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03ddab8b-ea2b-55eb-b6af-9fc42380d8ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2,3\\pm \\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238116} {:answer/id #uuid "0c4923f7-6dbe-533f-8fc9-cd13423241ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,3\\pm \\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238167} {:answer/id #uuid "15f92b3d-72a2-5edf-8070-6e3c6616c37c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,0,3-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238239} {:answer/id #uuid "219adb45-cc4a-595d-8997-8c9e5569e301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 2,3-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238347} {:answer/id #uuid "7310bcc0-1b4c-5dac-8f9c-c7809bf84977" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,3-\\text{i},1+3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c4923f7-6dbe-533f-8fc9-cd13423241ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,3\\pm \\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238167} :answer-field/id #uuid "ece2ff83-0d7a-55e7-848c-06928bf3c946" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0788e647-7a4e-5549-adea-a1cd056a2776" :question/math-academy-id "q-104514" :question/problem "Find all of the solutions to ${x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-2x+20=0$ given that $x=3-\\text{i}$ is a solution." :question/worked-solution "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-2x+20.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=3-\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{3-\\text{i}}=3+\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$(x-(3-\\text{i}))(x-(3+\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(3-\\text{i}))(x-(3+\\text{i})) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-(3-\\text{i}+(3+\\text{i)})x+(3-\\text{i})(3+\\text{i}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+({3}^{2}-(\\text{i})^{2}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+10 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}-6x+10$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,x+2 \\\\  & {x}^{2}-6x+10\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-\\phantom{2}2x+20} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2{x}^{2}-12x+20 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}-12x+20}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n     \n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}-6x+10)(x+2).$ \n\nTo find the remaining root, we set the linear factor equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2=0\\,⟹\\,x=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=-2,3\\pm \\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240892 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175963 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9dc69a9e-6344-5a92-beb7-0c9fef8171d3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Cubic Equation With Complex Roots Given One Complex Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aea373f-7c96-5b68-9731-2e656356b158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4,0,-5+2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238158} {:answer/id #uuid "0e72db85-3f8c-58b9-8b51-13c25222f1dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,4,-5+2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238180} {:answer/id #uuid "3a5ebd82-7075-5092-81f4-f465a40268f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 4,-5+2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238564} {:answer/id #uuid "9ddb6547-8aaf-51f7-9078-f4201f415cdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4,-5\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239434} {:answer/id #uuid "dfbf0afb-1527-5fe1-a5fa-a1efaa5f2f47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4,-5\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240010}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ddb6547-8aaf-51f7-9078-f4201f415cdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4,-5\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239434} :answer-field/id #uuid "45673382-dcde-536a-a583-e028748bc7e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d1be54b7-7530-59ba-90a4-e2866a7b30e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-104516" :question/problem "Find all of the solutions to ${x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-11x-116=0$ given that $x=-5+2\\text{i}$ is a solution." :question/worked-solution "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-11x-116.$\n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=-5+2\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{-5+2\\text{i}}=-5-2\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$(x-(-5+2\\text{i}))(x-(-5-2\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(-5+2\\text{i}))(x-(-5-2\\text{i})) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-(-5+2\\text{i}+(-5-2\\text{i)})x+(-5+2\\text{i})(-5-2\\text{i}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+10x+((-5)^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+10x+29 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}+10x+29$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,x-4 \\\\  & {x}^{2}+10x+29\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,{x}^{3}+\\phantom{1}6{x}^{2}-11x-116} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{3}+10{x}^{2}+29x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,-4{x}^{2}+40x-116 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,{-4{x}^{2}+40x-116}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n        \n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}+10x+29)(x-4).$ \n\nTo find the remaining root, we set the linear factor equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-4=0\\,⟹\\,x=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=4,-5\\pm 2\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198246 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38bfb48e-9b66-58a8-966a-fb6938e08073" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation That Determines a Minimum Distance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87a1b81f-6a01-51e3-8077-b35d6d3c965e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399984} {:answer/id #uuid "121d4c99-f9fd-560e-b8d1-8cf12f76ef01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399985} {:answer/id #uuid "37c5a8a6-835d-5948-a4e4-bc4371e503af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399986} {:answer/id #uuid "ed218258-9b28-520e-b77f-948add6059b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399987} {:answer/id #uuid "1a5f6dc5-0cb2-55ae-b3d1-11c8ecd253e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "121d4c99-f9fd-560e-b8d1-8cf12f76ef01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399985} :answer-field/id #uuid "e83f0ff3-bc35-5721-8d0b-7a1f7fc531ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0024a60-f6e5-5b56-9272-60dda6ebe058" :question/math-academy-id "q-118389" :question/problem "![](images/37/37434a61262f829160fc2b19fcadb67745a024d589cd1ce318ca4ab90d6ab2d9.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y=\\frac{x+1}{\\sqrt{x}}$ that is closest to the point $(2,0)$ satisfies the equation\n\n$$\n2x-3-\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}=0.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(2,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-2)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\frac{x+1}{\\sqrt{x}}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+{(\\frac{x+1}{\\sqrt{x}})}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x+1}{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+x+2+\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-3x+\\frac{1}{x}+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-3-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266081 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115250 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a4b135f-df0d-5443-bb93-796f32c380c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix Calculated Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c5e09aa-a71a-5bc1-abdb-ffbee8ae45ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263934} {:answer/id #uuid "7b00896b-7411-5bea-b442-6e238235690f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264066} {:answer/id #uuid "7c1c69e8-11c6-5242-a605-cf735a7f46aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264072} {:answer/id #uuid "967a322b-3f8a-5022-9edc-92691060c3ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264286} {:answer/id #uuid "ee11f2f0-386f-5dd5-b7b7-a51ec3fc0b57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b00896b-7411-5bea-b442-6e238235690f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264066} :answer-field/id #uuid "10fa802d-e4cb-5576-8c4b-61c19f125b76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e343a4f-de97-54f0-9ea2-a4b90179e66c" :question/math-academy-id "q-131380" :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${A}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${A}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115356 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "553f7b9d-c387-59b0-a0d6-9624d012e414" :knowledge-point/title "Domains of Functions With Trigonometric Components"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8b48905-c922-5f0b-904d-031f2f366d2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401366} {:answer/id #uuid "07837613-c6cb-599f-a1fe-60f06f0661aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401367} {:answer/id #uuid "823a0eb9-049b-54c7-87a1-4762c639c714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401368} {:answer/id #uuid "e5f740a3-7481-581f-876f-a404eea190e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401369} {:answer/id #uuid "730966c2-9ffc-5f1d-9533-075afe811e99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401370}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "730966c2-9ffc-5f1d-9533-075afe811e99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401370} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2067c3d-0d1e-5094-9c41-cc29fa1d1f8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c16eaa20-dd31-5858-a148-07284cbf2219" :question/math-academy-id "q-162632" :question/problem "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=2{t}^{3}\\,i+(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\bbox[5pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $2{t}^{3}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$, $t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401378 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115356 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "553f7b9d-c387-59b0-a0d6-9624d012e414" :knowledge-point/title "Domains of Functions With Trigonometric Components"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ede8e16-494c-5ead-ba64-5838ef07def5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401380} {:answer/id #uuid "4bb8a9be-6f0a-565b-b6b2-11bc4789ac9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401381} {:answer/id #uuid "70995bca-c45d-5efe-9c86-d0ad7a6058b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401382} {:answer/id #uuid "50f624a9-d806-5717-a7e5-2f6fcc28ebd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401383} {:answer/id #uuid "2e3fe947-d5c0-5bd5-8ca5-786edda0e0d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401384}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e3fe947-d5c0-5bd5-8ca5-786edda0e0d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401384} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a56ee90-9fa8-5ebf-a7cd-271d63b533b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7adf6f23-c582-5c46-b1a7-7edbfe6210af" :question/math-academy-id "q-162637" :question/problem "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=(1-{t}^{3})\\,i+3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,j+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\bbox[5pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $1-{t}^{3}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$, $t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284691 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176079 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57987f7d-21e1-51cb-a745-a24f2b257f11" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All the Roots of a Quartic Equation Given Two Real Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37c2d07c-8c90-5937-8b5a-a8ba8b7eb797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282184} {:answer/id #uuid "38ffd391-97cd-57d8-9e5f-995eb969cff5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282188} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc92ae7-1b14-514c-8d1e-9f809150db28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282935} {:answer/id #uuid "a46b620a-08b0-5c95-bdec-5069cc592b85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283123} {:answer/id #uuid "c164eeb2-c8f3-5604-8472-44c06ab5b3ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38ffd391-97cd-57d8-9e5f-995eb969cff5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282188} :answer-field/id #uuid "536f22ca-a493-57de-8aa2-d2abbc04f2c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a900a23-838c-5d27-8f6a-b5914108d28f" :question/math-academy-id "q-17317" :question/problem "Given that $x=-1$ and $x=4$ are solutions to the equation ${x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}-12x-16=0,$ find the remaining solutions." :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}-12x-16.$ Since ${x}_{1}=-1$ and ${x}_{2}=4$ are roots of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem, \n\n$$\n(x+1)(x-4)={x}^{2}-3x-4\n$$\n\nis a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}-3x-4$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}+4 \\\\  & {x}^{2}-3x-4\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}\\phantom{+0x}\\,\\,\\,-12x-16\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}-12x-16 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{4{x}^{2}-12x-16}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}-3x-4)({x}^{2}+4).$\n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}+4=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=-1,\\,4,\\,\\pm 2\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299700 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115250 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a4b135f-df0d-5443-bb93-796f32c380c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix Calculated Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a987cf7-3d87-5d25-bd5c-dd880b1daa91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297782} {:answer/id #uuid "96bd62c8-0cca-5580-ae04-416b82048473" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298206} {:answer/id #uuid "bf3c5dd7-085b-57d7-9575-a9f3f0bf6441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298392} {:answer/id #uuid "c817c443-b57e-5f24-8265-b649a1f3ac77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298426} {:answer/id #uuid "d1bc9edf-5bdb-5311-bb9b-39a82ba608ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf3c5dd7-085b-57d7-9575-a9f3f0bf6441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298392} :answer-field/id #uuid "a66018ec-422c-5e0e-b376-357aef68a84e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299198}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5898f79-8d87-5e96-a0ad-da8516834a84" :question/math-academy-id "q-31871" :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${B}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[B\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $B$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $B$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[B\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${B}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403011 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198246 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38bfb48e-9b66-58a8-966a-fb6938e08073" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation That Determines a Minimum Distance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30d03c60-3151-59b6-b7b9-fd72aa928ebd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403013} {:answer/id #uuid "5f900a01-8dad-54ab-bb5e-4a84ad557187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}{sin}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403014} {:answer/id #uuid "a782534b-97b6-5072-b2d6-441797eca761" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403015} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc1f314-0209-5118-9c75-ea26340d8758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403016} {:answer/id #uuid "704fb728-1e9d-5aae-b2b0-2c48902cd828" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403017}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a782534b-97b6-5072-b2d6-441797eca761" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403015} :answer-field/id #uuid "606ee288-277b-5ad8-a08e-806ce45db66b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb0ef12c-9b61-582b-acd5-7493f7def056" :question/math-academy-id "q-49054" :question/problem "![](images/47/47849c87c3c8cf9008b6531e740c6fc57058905cf17e31e4c93c479f337f0fbd.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y={e}^{x}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ that is closest to the point $(1,0)$ satisfies the equation\n\n$$\n2x-2+\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}=0.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(1,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y={e}^{x}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{({e}^{x}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-2+{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ 2x-2+{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403947 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092187 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d0cda54-4a4b-5255-b375-ce5e7b604dff" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Matrix Equation Using Algebraic Manipulation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53d8f566-865b-5d1d-968f-ffc7aeaaf0b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${D}^{-1}$ and ${I}_{n}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403949} {:answer/id #uuid "6898f8cf-ef60-599d-b703-aeb35e14e141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D$ and ${I}_{n}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403950} {:answer/id #uuid "0329ed6f-dee9-5205-b238-18984ed73972" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${D}^{-1}$ and $D$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403951} {:answer/id #uuid "a15466de-3c65-5970-8e36-5b25e21ca209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${D}^{-1}$ and ${D}^{-1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403952} {:answer/id #uuid "daabc2d4-f4ce-50f8-9f41-a9e7585ac6e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D$ and $z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53d8f566-865b-5d1d-968f-ffc7aeaaf0b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${D}^{-1}$ and ${I}_{n}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403949} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cf7d7a2-cf8f-5d79-a5a3-2c7fb6abb055" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "863bd08e-01cd-5a69-b3ce-bacc176f8b4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-78559" :question/problem "Consider a system of $n$ linear equations in $n$ variables, written in matrix form as $D\\,z=c.$ Here, $D$ is the non-singular coefficient matrix of the system, $z$ is the column vector containing the variables, and $c$ is the column vector containing the constants. The unique solution of the system can be found using the method shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D\\,z & =c \\\\ {D}^{-1}D\\,z & =\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\color{blue}{\\phantom{{D}^{-1}}}}c \\\\ \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\color{blue}{\\phantom{{I}_{n}}}}z & ={D}^{-1}\\,c \\\\ z & ={D}^{-1}\\,c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom top to bottom and right to left, what are the missing parts of the derivation?" :question/worked-solution "Since $D$ is not singular, it must have an inverse ${D}^{-1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $D\\,z=c$ by ${D}^{-1},$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D\\,z & =c \\\\ {D}^{-1}D\\,z & ={D}^{-1}c \\\\ {I}_{n}\\,z & ={D}^{-1}\\,c \\\\ z & ={D}^{-1}\\,c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts are ${D}^{-1}$ and ${I}_{n}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403954 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092187 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d0cda54-4a4b-5255-b375-ce5e7b604dff" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Matrix Equation Using Algebraic Manipulation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f916d57-a48d-5f42-a1e6-2cc8f554dc1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${D}^{-1}$ and $q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403956} {:answer/id #uuid "a2636cd7-9981-5e94-8ce6-ffc7ac605d47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${D}^{-1}$ and $t$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403957} {:answer/id #uuid "cd887cdb-1f42-5d46-bc3e-089ab176ac9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D$ and ${I}_{n}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403958} {:answer/id #uuid "e14838e1-6770-514b-a8d3-264fece5dc27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${D}^{-1}$ and $D$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403959} {:answer/id #uuid "45769dbd-ae3d-5976-b913-0ac9c72c7a88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D$ and $q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f916d57-a48d-5f42-a1e6-2cc8f554dc1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${D}^{-1}$ and $q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403956} :answer-field/id #uuid "96efe5ef-8066-599a-a9a3-934df32a5fcd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ecf8e0a5-c1dd-5243-9116-de59a516e7ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-78561" :question/problem "Consider a system of $n$ linear equations in $n$ variables, written in matrix form as $D\\,t=q.$ Here, $D$ is the non-singular coefficient matrix of the system, $t$ is the column vector containing the variables, and $q$ is the column vector containing the constants. The unique solution of the system can be found using the method shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D\\,t & =q \\\\ \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\color{blue}{\\phantom{{D}^{-1}}}}D\\,t & ={D}^{-1}\\,q \\\\ {I}_{n}\\,t & ={D}^{-1}\\,\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\color{blue}{\\phantom{\\mathbf{q}}}} \\\\ t & ={D}^{-1}\\,q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom top to bottom and left to right, what are the missing parts of the derivation?" :question/worked-solution "Since $D$ is not singular, it must have an inverse ${D}^{-1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $D\\,t=q$ by ${D}^{-1},$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D\\,t & =q \\\\ {D}^{-1}D\\,t & ={D}^{-1}\\,q \\\\ {I}_{n}\\,t & ={D}^{-1}\\,q \\\\ t & ={D}^{-1}\\,q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts are ${D}^{-1}$ and $q.$"}]}
